\title{The Cohomology of Split-View Lies:\\
\large Certified Detection and Witness Locality for Coordination Faults in Multi-Agent Swarms}
\author{Erich Owens \\ \texttt{erich@curiositech.ai} \\ Curiositech AI Research}
\date{September 2026 \\ \small Draft 2 --- supersedes the September 2026 ``Cohomology of Swarms'' draft}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
An observer that sees only pairwise disagreements between agents in a swarm can certify, from the topology of the communication graph and one sparse linear solve, that some agent has told different neighbours different things. This paper states exactly that and no more. We restrict the agent state to claims about named, finite-valued \emph{facts} (which commit is at the head of a branch, who holds a lock on a symbol, what verdict a reviewer gave), encode each claim one-hot, and define the observed 1-cochain from the messages actually exchanged on each channel rather than from any latent agent state. Under this encoding the completion residual $r$ of the sheaf Laplacian counts irreconcilable split-views in units of $\sqrt 2$, needs no noise model on discrete coordinates, and inherits the partial-visibility guarantees of the Harbor Paper 7 residual: $r>0$ is a proof, tree-shaped swarms are blind, and a single split-view on edge $e$ produces $r=\sqrt{2}\sqrt{1-\Reff(e)}$. We then replace the previous draft's declared ``review contract'' 2-cells with the canonical clique complex of the communication graph, which makes the Hodge decomposition of the inconsistency a graph invariant rather than a modelling choice. In that setting the harmonic component has a precise meaning: it is the part of the inconsistency not witnessed by any triangle. We prove that it vanishes identically on chordal communication graphs, that under a team-chordality design rule every cycle carrying harmonic circulation crosses a team boundary, and that for a single split-view the curl/harmonic split is a per-edge topological constant that can be tabulated offline. We also state plainly what the method cannot see: an agent that lies consistently to everyone is a gauge transformation and is invisible in every topology, and an honest partition in which each side is internally consistent produces $r=0$. A 190-line reference implementation reproduces every numerical claim in the paper.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{What this paper claims}

Multi-agent software systems---fleets of LLM agents editing a shared codebase, reviewing each other's pull requests, and gossiping state across asynchronous channels---fail in a specific way that log inspection is bad at catching: an agent reports one thing on one channel and a contradictory thing on another. A Manager tells the Producer that the Critic approved; the Critic tells the Producer it rejected. A worker tells its supervisor the tests passed and tells its peer that they are still running. We call this a \emph{split-view lie}. It need not be malicious: prompt drift, stale context, or a hallucinated tool result all produce it.

The previous draft of this work (``The Cohomology of Swarms'') claimed that cellular sheaf cohomology detects, triages, and repairs ``coordination inconsistency'' in general, and that a scalar \emph{Swarm Legibility Ratio} $L(g)\in[0,1]$ distinguishes ``local review hallucinations'' ($L\approx 0$) from ``macro-network partitions'' ($L\approx 1$). Those claims were too strong in three ways, and this draft is the result of retracting them to what can be proved.

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{What is detected.} The sheaf residual sees exactly one fault class: split-view lies. An agent that tells every neighbour the same false thing is a \emph{gauge transformation} of the observed cochain and is invisible in every topology (Theorem~\ref{thm:consistent-invisible}). The previous draft's ``hallucinated sign-offs'' are detectable only when the sign-off is reported inconsistently.
\item \textbf{What the stalk contains.} The previous draft folded SHA-256 digests, Lamport clocks, token budgets, and review votes into a single real vector. Differences of hashes have no metric, clocks legitimately skew, and a vote is an agent's opinion rather than a shared fact, so the resulting residuals (e.g.\ $r = 806{,}404$ in its live trial) measured the encoding rather than the swarm. Here the stalk is a one-hot encoding of claims about named finite-valued facts (\S\ref{sec:model}). Residual energy is then a weighted count of disagreements and needs no noise model.
\item \textbf{What the 2-cells mean.} The previous draft's Hodge split depended on which agent triples the operator declared as ``review contracts'': fill every cycle and $L\equiv 0$, fill none and $L\equiv 1$. Its case studies were consequences of that choice. Here the 2-complex is the clique complex of the communication graph, which is determined by the graph alone (\S\ref{sec:locality}). The harmonic component then means ``inconsistency not witnessed by any triangle'', and its relationship to team structure is a theorem with a stated hypothesis (Theorem~\ref{thm:boundary}) rather than an interpretation.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Contributions}

\begin{enumerate}
\item A \emph{message-cochain} model in which the observed 1-cochain is built from what agents said on each channel, not from a latent state vector. This is the only construction under which the cochain can fail to be exact, and it makes precise that the detector's object is the split-view (\S\ref{sec:model}).
\item A one-hot fact encoding under which $\|g_e\|\in\{0,\sqrt 2\}$ per fact, faithfulness is exact with margin $\sqrt 2$, and the residual is a count (Proposition~\ref{prop:norm}).
\item The partial-visibility detection results of Paper~7 \cite{owens7} restated for this encoding, including the closed form $r=\sqrt{2}\sqrt{1-\Reff(e)}$ for a single split-view (Theorem~\ref{thm:cr1}).
\item \emph{Witness locality}: on the clique complex, the Hodge decomposition of the inconsistency is canonical. We prove (i) $H^1(\Cl(G))=0$ for chordal $G$, so the harmonic part vanishes; (ii) under team-chordality, every cycle carrying harmonic circulation crosses a team boundary; (iii) a split-view between two neighbours of one agent produces triangle curl if and only if those neighbours are adjacent (\S\ref{sec:locality}).
\item A per-edge \emph{edge legibility} $L(e)=H_{ee}/(1-\Reff(e))$, a topological constant, such that any single split-view on $e$ has Swarm Legibility Ratio exactly $L(e)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:edge-legibility}). The operator can tabulate this offline and know, before any fault occurs, how each channel's faults will present.
\item The greedy repair controller of the previous draft, kept with its cycle-rank termination bound, plus an observation from the reference implementation that the energy-to-cost rule can isolate a vertex rather than cut the offending bridge when bridge costs are high (\S\ref{sec:repair}).
\item A reference implementation of under 200 lines whose output is reproduced in \S\ref{sec:eval}. No number in this paper is asserted without having been computed by it.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{A worked example}

Four agents $A,B,C,D$ share a fact $f$ = ``head commit of branch \texttt{main}'' with values in $\{\mathtt{a1},\mathtt{b2}\}$. The channels form a 4-cycle $A\!-\!B\!-\!C\!-\!D\!-\!A$. Every agent tells its two neighbours $\mathtt{a1}$, except that $A$ tells $D$ it is $\mathtt{b2}$. The observer sees, per channel, only the pair of claims exchanged: three channels agree, channel $DA$ disagrees. A single disagreeing channel is not evidence of a lie---$A$ and $D$ might simply be at different commits. But the four channels together are: if $A$ held $\mathtt{b2}$ then channel $AB$ should disagree, and it does not. No assignment of one claim per agent explains all four channels. The residual is $r=\sqrt{2}\sqrt{1-3/4}=\sqrt{2}/2$, and it is a certificate, not a heuristic: a positive residual means no consistent-per-agent story exists.

Remove channel $CD$ and the cycle opens. Now $A$'s split-view is explained by ``$A$ and $D$ are at different commits'' and $r=0$. The lie is still there; the topology no longer traps it. This is the entire content of the method. Everything below makes it precise, partial-visibility-safe, and localisable.

\section{Preliminaries}\label{sec:prelim}

Let $G=(V,E)$ be a finite simple graph, $n=|V|$, $m=|E|$, with each edge given the orientation $u\to v$ for $u<v$. The incidence matrix $B\in\R^{m\times n}$ has $B_{e,u}=-1$, $B_{e,v}=+1$ for $e=(u,v)$. We write $\delta_0=B$ for the coboundary $C^0\to C^1$, so $(\delta_0 x)_e = x_v-x_u$. The cycle space is $Z_1=\ker B^\top$, and $\R^m=\im\delta_0\oplus\ker\delta_0^\top$ orthogonally. The projector onto the cycle space is $\Pi = I - BB^+$, and $\Pi_{ee} = 1-\Reff(e)$ where $\Reff(e)=(\mathbf 1_v-\mathbf 1_u)^\top L^+(\mathbf 1_v-\mathbf 1_u)$ is the effective resistance of $e$ in the unit-resistor network, $L=B^\top B$ \cite{hansen-ghrist}. The first Betti number is $\beta_1(G)=m-n+c(G)$ with $c$ the number of components.

The \emph{clique complex} $\Cl(G)$ is the simplicial complex whose $k$-simplices are the $(k{+}1)$-cliques of $G$. We use only its 2-skeleton: vertices, edges, and the set $F$ of triangles. The coboundary $\delta_1:C^1\to C^2$ is $(\delta_1 g)_{uvw}=g_{uv}+g_{vw}-g_{uw}$ for $u<v<w$, and $\delta_1\delta_0=0$. The Hodge 1-Laplacian is $L_1=\delta_0\delta_0^\top+\delta_1^\top\delta_1$ and $C^1=\im\delta_0\oplus\ker L_1\oplus\im\delta_1^\top$ orthogonally, with $\ker L_1\cong H^1(\Cl(G);\R)$ \cite{hansen-ghrist}. We write $P_{\mathrm{grad}},P_{\mathrm{harm}},P_{\mathrm{curl}}$ for the three orthogonal projectors; $\Pi=P_{\mathrm{harm}}+P_{\mathrm{curl}}$.

A graph is \emph{chordal} if every cycle of length $\ge 4$ has a chord. Every chordal graph has a \emph{simplicial vertex}, one whose neighbourhood is a clique \cite{dirac}.

\section{Model: Facts, Claims, and Message Cochains}\label{sec:model}

\subsection{Facts and claims}

\begin{definition}[Fact registry]
A fact registry is a finite set $\mathcal F$ of fact names, each with a finite value set $\mathrm{Val}(f)$. Examples: $\mathrm{head}(B)\in\{\text{commit digests}\}$, $\mathrm{holder}(s)\in V\cup\{\bot\}$ for an AST symbol $s$, $\mathrm{verdict}(w,p)\in\{-1,0,+1\}$ for reviewer $w$ on pull request $p$, $\mathrm{status}(t)\in\{\mathrm{pass},\mathrm{fail},\mathrm{error}\}$ for test $t$.
\end{definition}

A fact is something two agents \emph{should} agree about. An agent's own review vote is not a fact in this sense---two agents may legitimately hold different opinions---but ``what verdict agent $w$ gave'' is: it has one true value and any two agents reporting it should report the same one. This distinction fixes the previous draft's mis-modelled review triad (\S\ref{sec:triad}).

Coordinates that legitimately differ across agents are excluded from the registry: logical clocks, token spend, and free-text rationale embeddings. Bounded clock skew is an inequality constraint and belongs to a temporal-logic layer, not to a sheaf whose consistency condition is equality.

\subsection{Messages and the observed cochain}

The observed data is what was said, not what was believed.

\begin{definition}[Message table]
For each ordered pair $(v,u)$ with $\{u,v\}\in E$ and each fact $f$, let $m_{v\to u}(f)\in\mathrm{Val}(f)\cup\{\bot\}$ be the claim $v$ sent to $u$ about $f$ in the observation window, with $\bot$ meaning $v$ said nothing about $f$ to $u$.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Message cochain]
Fix an ordering of $\mathrm{Val}(f)$ and write $\onehot{a}\in\R^{|\mathrm{Val}(f)|}$ for the indicator of value $a$. The message cochain $g\in C^1(G;\R^{D})$, $D=\sum_f|\mathrm{Val}(f)|$, has, on edge $e=(u,v)$ with $u<v$ and in the block for fact $f$,
\[
g_e^{(f)}=\begin{cases}\onehot{m_{v\to u}(f)}-\onehot{m_{u\to v}(f)} & \text{if both are }\neq\bot,\\ 0 & \text{otherwise.}\end{cases}
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Unit of disagreement]\label{prop:norm}
$\|g_e^{(f)}\|_2\in\{0,\sqrt 2\}$, with $\sqrt 2$ if and only if the two endpoints reported different values of $f$ to each other.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The difference of two distinct one-hot vectors has one $+1$ and one $-1$ entry.
\end{proof}

Because the encoding is blockwise per fact and per value, $\delta_0$ block-diagonalises into $D$ scalar copies of $B$, and every quantity below decomposes as a sum over facts. We suppress the fact index where it is not needed.

\begin{remark}[Why messages, not states]
If $g$ were computed from a latent state $x_v$ as $g_e=P_e x_v-P_e x_u$, then $g=\delta_0 x$ by construction and the residual would vanish identically. The cochain can be non-exact only because each directed message is an independent report. This is the model of Paper~7 \cite{owens7} and it is the only one under which the machinery does anything.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Split-views and gauge}

\begin{definition}[Consistent agent, split-view]
Agent $v$ is \emph{consistent} on fact $f$ if $m_{v\to u}(f)$ takes a single value over all $u$ with $m_{v\to u}(f)\neq\bot$. Otherwise $v$ has a \emph{split-view} on $f$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Consistent swarms are gauge]\label{thm:consistent-invisible}
If every agent is consistent on $f$, then $g^{(f)}\in\im\delta_0$ and the completion residual on $f$ is zero, in every topology and under every visibility pattern.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Let $c_v$ be $v$'s single claim (arbitrary if $v$ never spoke). Set $x_v=\onehot{c_v}$. Then $g_e=\onehot{c_v}-\onehot{c_u}=(\delta_0 x)_e$ on every edge where both spoke, and $0=(\delta_0 x)_e$ is not required on edges where one was silent since those entries of $g$ were set to zero; restrict $\delta_0$ to the spoken edge set $K$ and the identity holds there.
\end{proof}

The contrapositive is the detector's soundness statement, and it deserves the emphasis the previous draft did not give it: \textbf{a positive residual proves that some agent told different neighbours different things}. It does not prove which agent, and it does not detect an agent that is wrong but consistent. A Tester that tells everyone the suite passed when it failed produces $r=0$. That is a limitation of any detector that sees only disagreements; it is not a limitation of this particular one.

\begin{theorem}[Honest partition is gauge]\label{thm:partition-gauge}
Let $V=V_1\sqcup V_2$, every agent in $V_1$ consistently claim $a$ and every agent in $V_2$ consistently claim $b\neq a$. Then $r=0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Immediate from Theorem~\ref{thm:consistent-invisible}.
\end{proof}

This retracts the previous draft's ``macro-network partition cavity'' interpretation. Two teams that have drifted into different but internally consistent realities are exactly the gauge case: every bridge channel disagrees, and the disagreement is explained by a potential. What the harmonic component detects is stated correctly in \S\ref{sec:locality}: a split-view whose contradicted neighbours are not adjacent.

\section{Detection under Partial Visibility}\label{sec:detect}

The observer sees $g_K$ on a visible edge set $K\subseteq E$ and nothing on $E\setminus K$. Write $B_K$, $\Pi_K$, $\Reff^K$ for the corresponding objects on $G_K=(V,K)$. The completion residual is
\[
\rho=\Pi_K g_K,\qquad r=\|\rho\|_2=\min_{x}\|g_K-B_Kx\|_2 .
\]

\begin{theorem}[Paper~7 residual, one-hot form]\label{thm:cr1}
\begin{enumerate}
\item $r>0$ if and only if no assignment of one real vector per agent explains every visible channel; in particular $r>0$ implies some agent has a split-view (Theorem~\ref{thm:consistent-invisible}).
\item If $g_K=\delta_0 x+\varepsilon_K$ with $x$ the consistent-claim potential, then $\|\varepsilon_K\|_2\ge r$.
\item (Single split-view.) If exactly one agent $v$ has a split-view, on exactly one fact, disagreeing with exactly one neighbour $u$ (so $\varepsilon_K$ is supported on $e=(u,v)$ with $\|\varepsilon_e\|=\sqrt 2$), then
\[
r=\sqrt{2}\,\sqrt{1-\Reff^K(e)} .
\]
\item If $e$ is a bridge of $G_K$ then $\Reff^K(e)=1$ and $r=0$: a split-view across a cut edge is invisible.
\item $B_K^\top\rho=0$; $\rho$ is a circulation, and its support lies on cycles of $G_K$ through the split-viewing agent.
\end{enumerate}
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Items 1, 2, 4, 5 are Theorems CR-1 and CR-2 of \cite{owens7} verbatim; the one-hot encoding changes nothing in the linear algebra. For item 3, $\varepsilon_K=\varepsilon_e\otimes\mathbf 1_e$ with $\|\varepsilon_e\|=\sqrt2$, and $\|\Pi_K(\varepsilon_e\otimes\mathbf 1_e)\|^2=\|\varepsilon_e\|^2(\Pi_K)_{ee}=2(1-\Reff^K(e))$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Tree blindness]
If $G_K$ is a forest then $\Pi_K=0$ and $r\equiv 0$ for every message table. Hierarchical supervisor topologies (LangGraph supervisor, CrewAI hierarchical, AutoGen manager) are forests and cannot detect split-views without added cross-links.
\end{corollary}

\begin{remark}[No noise model on discrete facts]
The previous draft's sub-Gaussian threshold $\tau(\sigma,\delta)$ existed to separate real-valued jitter from lies. One-hot claims about discrete facts have no jitter: either the two ends said the same value or they did not. The decision rule is $r>0$, tested against solver tolerance only. A stochastic model is needed only if real-valued coordinates are reintroduced, and we recommend against reintroducing them.
\end{remark}

\section{Witness Locality on the Clique Complex}\label{sec:locality}

A positive residual certifies a split-view. The next question is \emph{how local the witness is}: is the contradiction visible on a triangle, or only on a long cycle? This is a question about the graph, and the clique complex answers it without any declared contracts.

\subsection{The Hodge split of the inconsistency}

Take $F$ = all triangles of $G_K$. Decompose the inconsistency $\rho=g_K-P_{\mathrm{grad}}g_K$ as
\[
\rho = \underbrace{P_{\mathrm{curl}}\,g_K}_{\text{triangle-witnessed}} + \underbrace{P_{\mathrm{harm}}\,g_K}_{\text{unwitnessed by triangles}},
\qquad
L(g)=\frac{\|P_{\mathrm{harm}}g_K\|^2}{\|P_{\mathrm{harm}}g_K\|^2+\|P_{\mathrm{curl}}g_K\|^2}.
\]
$L(g)$ is the previous draft's Swarm Legibility Ratio, now defined on a canonical complex. Two objects carry more information than $L$ and should be inspected first:
\begin{itemize}
\item the per-triangle curl $\delta_1 g_K\in C^2$, whose non-zero entries \emph{name} the triangles on which three agents' six messages cannot be reconciled;
\item the harmonic part $h=P_{\mathrm{harm}}g_K$, whose circulation on a cycle $C$, $\langle h,\mathbf 1_C\rangle$, identifies the long cycles carrying unwitnessed inconsistency.
\end{itemize}

\subsection{Chordal graphs have no harmonic part}

\begin{theorem}\label{thm:chordal}
If $G$ is chordal and connected then $\Cl(G)$ is contractible; in particular $H^1(\Cl(G);\R)=0$, $\ker L_1=0$, and $L(g)=0$ for every message table with $r>0$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Induct on $|V|$. A chordal graph has a simplicial vertex $v$ \cite{dirac}; its neighbourhood $N(v)$ is a clique, so the star of $v$ in $\Cl(G)$ is the cone on the simplex spanned by $N(v)$, and $\Cl(G)$ deformation-retracts onto $\Cl(G-v)$. Induced subgraphs of chordal graphs are chordal, and $G-v$ is connected if $|V|\ge 2$ (a simplicial vertex is not a cut vertex). The base case is a point.
\end{proof}

Every inconsistency in a chordal swarm is triangle-witnessed. This is a design rule: if intra-team communication is arranged so that each team's induced graph is chordal (a clique, a fan, a triangulated ring), then within a team every split-view names its triangles.

\subsection{Team boundaries}

\begin{definition}[Team-chordal swarm]
A partition $V=\bigsqcup_i T_i$ into teams is \emph{team-chordal} if each induced subgraph $G[T_i]$ is chordal and connected. Edges with endpoints in different teams are \emph{boundary edges}.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Harmonic circulation crosses team boundaries]\label{thm:boundary}
In a team-chordal swarm, let $h\in\ker L_1$. Then $\langle h,\mathbf 1_C\rangle=0$ for every cycle $C$ contained in a single team. Consequently every cycle with non-zero harmonic circulation contains a boundary edge.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
$C$ lies in $G[T_i]$, whose clique complex is contractible (Theorem~\ref{thm:chordal}), so $H_1(\Cl(G[T_i]);\R)=0$ and the 1-chain $\mathbf 1_C$ is a boundary: $\mathbf 1_C=\partial_2\sigma$ for a 2-chain $\sigma$ supported on triangles inside $T_i$. Those triangles are triangles of $\Cl(G)$. Then $\langle h,\mathbf 1_C\rangle=\langle h,\partial_2\sigma\rangle=\langle\delta_1 h,\sigma\rangle=0$ since $h\in\ker\delta_1$.
\end{proof}

This is the correct form of ``$L\approx 1$ means a boundary is involved''. Note what it does not say: it does not say the harmonic part is \emph{supported} on boundary edges (in the reference example it flows through the teams), and it does not say the lying agent sits on a boundary. It says any cycle that witnesses the unwitnessed part crosses a boundary.

\subsection{When a split-view is triangle-witnessed}

\begin{theorem}[Triangle witness criterion]\label{thm:triangle}
Suppose agent $v$ has a split-view on $f$, telling neighbour $u$ value $a$ and every other neighbour value $b\neq a$, and every other agent is consistent. Then $(\delta_1 g)_{\tau}\neq 0$ for a triangle $\tau$ if and only if $\tau=\{u,v,w\}$ with $w\in N(u)\cap N(v)$. In particular the curl part is non-zero if and only if $u$ and some other neighbour of $v$ are adjacent.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Write $g=\delta_0 x+\varepsilon$ with $x_v=\onehot{b}$ the consistent potential and $\varepsilon$ supported on $e=(u,v)$, $\varepsilon_e=\pm(\onehot{a}-\onehot{b})$. Then $\delta_1 g=\delta_1\varepsilon$, which is non-zero exactly on triangles containing $e$, i.e.\ $\{u,v,w\}$ with $w$ adjacent to both. $P_{\mathrm{curl}}\,g=P_{\mathrm{curl}}\,\varepsilon\neq0$ iff $\delta_1\varepsilon\neq 0$.
\end{proof}

So the dichotomy the previous draft attributed to fault type is in fact a property of the split-viewing agent's neighbourhood: the contradicted neighbour $u$ either shares a further neighbour with $v$ (triangle-witnessed) or does not (witnessed only on cycles of length $\ge 4$). Bridge channels between teams are the canonical case of the latter.

\subsection{Edge legibility is a topological constant}

\begin{theorem}[Edge legibility]\label{thm:edge-legibility}
For a single split-view supported on edge $e$ with perturbation $\varepsilon=\varepsilon_e\otimes\mathbf 1_e$, the three Hodge energies are
\[
\|P_{\mathrm{grad}}\varepsilon\|^2=\|\varepsilon_e\|^2\,\Reff(e),\quad
\|P_{\mathrm{curl}}\varepsilon\|^2=\|\varepsilon_e\|^2\,C_{ee},\quad
\|P_{\mathrm{harm}}\varepsilon\|^2=\|\varepsilon_e\|^2\,H_{ee},
\]
with $C=P_{\mathrm{curl}}$, $H=P_{\mathrm{harm}}$, and $\Reff(e)+C_{ee}+H_{ee}=1$. Hence the Swarm Legibility Ratio of any single split-view on $e$ equals
\[
L(e)\;=\;\frac{H_{ee}}{1-\Reff(e)}\;=\;\frac{H_{ee}}{H_{ee}+C_{ee}},
\]
independent of the fact, the values, and the magnitude.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
For an orthogonal projector $P$, $\|P(\varepsilon_e\otimes\mathbf 1_e)\|^2=\|\varepsilon_e\|^2 P_{ee}$, and the three projectors sum to the identity.
\end{proof}

$L(e)$ can be computed for every channel before the swarm runs. It tells the operator, per channel, what fraction of a fault on that channel will be triangle-witnessed. In the reference topology of \S\ref{sec:eval}, intra-team edges have $L(e)\le 0.14$ and boundary edges have $L(e)=1$. This is the concrete ``structure determined from topology'' that the previous draft asserted but did not supply.

\subsection{The review triad, correctly modelled}\label{sec:triad}

Let Producer $P$, Critic $C$, Manager $M$ form a triangle, and let the fact be $f=\mathrm{verdict}(C,\mathrm{pr}17)\in\{-1,0,+1\}$. The Critic tells both $P$ and $M$ that its verdict is $-1$. The Manager tells $P$ that the Critic's verdict is $+1$ and tells $C$ nothing about $f$ (or tells $C$ the truth). On edge $PM$ the claims are $M\to P{:}\,{+1}$, $P\to M{:}\,{-1}$ (Producer relays what it heard from Critic); on $PC$ and $CM$ the claims agree. Then $g$ is a single split-view on edge $PM$ contradicted by the triangle, $(\delta_1 g)_{PCM}\neq 0$, and $L(g)=L(PM)=0$ since $K_3$ is chordal. The triangle names itself. Nothing about votes as stalk coordinates is required.

\section{Repair}\label{sec:repair}

Given costs $w(e)>0$, the previous draft's Greedy Energy-to-Cost Controller selects $e^\ast=\arg\max_e\|\rho_e\|^2/w(e)$, fences $e^\ast$, recomputes, and repeats. We keep the result:

\begin{theorem}[Termination, \cite{owens7} CR-4]
If $r>0$ the selected edge lies on a cycle, so fencing it reduces $\beta_1(G_K)$ by exactly one; the controller reaches $r=0$ in at most $\beta_1(G_K)$ rounds.
\end{theorem}

Two cautions from the reference implementation. First, the controller restores $r=0$ by destroying cycles, which is the same thing as destroying the swarm's ability to detect the next lie; ``repair'' here means quarantine of the witness, and the operator must re-close the loop afterwards. Second, the energy-to-cost rule is greedy and not cost-optimal: in \S\ref{sec:eval} with boundary edges priced at $5$ and team edges at $1$, the controller isolates the lying agent's bridge partner by cutting three cheap team edges (total cost $3$) instead of the single bridge (cost $5$). That is cheaper by the stated objective and arguably the wrong operational outcome; a cut-set formulation with connectivity constraints is the right follow-up.

\section{Implementation}\label{sec:impl}

What to log: for every message $v\to u$, the tuple $(v,u,f,\text{value})$ for each registered fact it asserts. What to compute per heartbeat: build $g$ (Definition~3.3), solve $L_K\hat x=B_K^\top g_K$ per coordinate (sparse Cholesky reused across ticks, or CG), $\rho=g_K-B_K\hat x$, $r=\|\rho\|$. If $r>0$: evaluate $\delta_1 g_K$ (a sparse matrix-vector product over the triangle list) and report the non-zero triangles; if residual energy remains after removing the curl part, report the harmonic circulation on a cycle basis. The per-edge table $\Reff(e),C_{ee},H_{ee},L(e)$ is computed once per topology change.

Complexity: $D$ scalar Laplacian solves, $\tilde O(|K|\cdot D)$ with near-linear SDD solvers \cite{spielman-teng}; the clique-complex curl is $O(|F|\cdot D)$ with $|F|$ the triangle count. One-hot inflates $D$ to $\sum_f|\mathrm{Val}(f)|$; for facts with large value sets (commit digests) it suffices to allocate one coordinate per value \emph{observed in the window}, which is at most $2|K|$ per fact.

The reference implementation (\texttt{swarmsheaf.py}, 190 lines, NumPy only) builds $B$, $\delta_1$, the three projectors, the edge table, the message cochain, the analysis, and the greedy controller. It uses dense pseudoinverses and is intended for correctness on swarms of up to a few hundred agents, not for production scale.

\section{Verification}\label{sec:eval}

